{
  "title": "Valid Parentheses",
  "description": "Check whether a bracket string is balanced and correctly nested.",
  "statement": "A compiler front-end needs to know that every opening bracket in a token stream is closed by the matching bracket, in the right order.\n\nYou are given a string built from the six bracket characters `(`, `)`, `[`, `]`, `{`, `}`.\n\nReport `true` when every bracket is closed by a bracket of the same kind and no bracket is closed before it is opened, otherwise report `false`. Nesting order matters: `([)]` is **not** balanced even though each bracket type appears as often as its partner.\n\nA stack is the natural tool - opening brackets are pushed, closing brackets must pop a matching opening bracket from the top.",
  "inputFormat": "The input is read from standard input as exactly one line:\n\n1. Line 1: the bracket string, containing only the characters `(`, `)`, `[`, `]`, `{`, `}` and nothing else. The line is never empty (`1 <= length <= 100000`).",
  "outputFormat": "Print a single line containing the lowercase word `true` if the bracket string is balanced, otherwise the lowercase word `false`.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= length of the string <= 10^5",
    "The string contains only `(`, `)`, `[`, `]`, `{`, `}`",
    "The input line is never empty"
  ],
  "notes": "Both failure modes must be caught: a closing bracket that does not match the innermost open bracket, and leftover opening brackets after the scan. Nesting can reach 500 levels in the test data, so recursion is unnecessary - and in some languages outright unsafe. Output must be lowercase.",
  "difficulty": "EASY",
  "tags": [
    "stack",
    "string"
  ],
  "timeLimitMs": 1000,
  "memoryLimitKb": 256000,
